Genetic engineering can disrupt ecosystems by introducing invasive species that outcompete native organisms, leading to biodiversity loss.
Unintended gene transfers between modified and wild species may create unpredictable ecological consequences.
Health risks from untested genetic modifications could include allergic reactions or long-term toxicity in humans and animals.
Lack of regulation may allow harmful traits to spread uncontrollably, threatening both environmental and public health.
History shows that even well-intentioned genetic modifications can have cascading effects that are difficult to reverse.